# Opening PRs

## Ask first, always

**Never commit, push, or open a PR unless the user asked for it in that turn.**
Finishing the code is the deliverable; landing it is a separate decision that is
theirs. "The change is done and validated" is where you stop and report, not a
cue to reach for `git commit`.

This holds even when the work obviously ends in a PR, even when a previous PR in
the same session was approved, and even when a skill or document you are
following describes opening one. Approval for one PR is not standing approval
for the next.

The one exception is an explicit standing instruction in the current
conversation ("open a PR for each of these as you go") -- and it expires with
that task, not the session.

## Branch naming

`sweinstein-<ticket-number>` -- the GitHub username, a hyphen, and the bare
Shortcut story number with no `sc-` prefix.

```bash
git fetch origin master
git checkout -b sweinstein-187147 origin/master
```

- `sweinstein-187147`, not `sweinstein/187147`, not `sc-187147`, not a
  description of the change.
- No ticket? Ask what to use rather than inventing a slug.
- This wins over `$AGENTD_BRANCH_PREFIX` and over any skill that interpolates
  it.
- Never push the agentd worktree branch (`ondemand-*`, `foundry-*`,
  `agentd-pool-*`) -- those are agentd's own bookkeeping. Cut the
  `sweinstein-...` branch for the PR instead.
- Never rename an existing PR's branch to conform; the PR and its reviews are
  bound to that head ref.

## PR title

`[sc-<ticket-number>] <Concise description>`

The `[sc-...]` bracket comes first, before any `[component]` tag.

## PR body

**First line is the Shortcut link, as a markdown link whose text is the
`sc-` id:**

```markdown
[sc-187147](https://app.shortcut.com/chronosphere/story/187147/clean-up-enable-time-range-snapping)
```

Then a blank line, then the body. Keep it succinct -- what the change is meant
to do and any noteworthy implementation choice, not an enumeration of the diff.

If the user hands you a link whose story number disagrees with the `sc-` id they
asked for in the title, say so rather than silently reconciling it.

## Draft is where a PR starts, not a state you put it back into

Open every PR as a draft (`gh pr create --draft`), whatever its size and however
finished it is. The user marks it ready to review themselves; do not un-draft
one on your own.

**Never convert a ready PR back to draft.** Not when you push further commits to
it, not when CI goes red, not when review feedback arrives, not when you find a
bug in your own work mid-turn. Once a PR has been marked ready -- by the user,
or by you because they asked in that turn -- it stays ready, and
`gh pr ready --undo` is not yours to run.

The flag is the author's signal to their reviewers, so flipping it silently
retracts a review request they made. "This needs more work" is what a comment on
the PR is for. Both directions are one rule: you set the flag when you create
the PR, or when told to, and never otherwise.

An instruction to leave a particular PR ready is durable for that PR. It does
not expire with the turn, and a later change to the same PR does not reopen the
question.

## What does not go in a PR

The `agent-self-identification` rule's signature line signs *messages*, not
*fields*. A PR title, a PR body, a commit message, and a branch name are fields:
none of them carry a `<model>: <context>:` prefix.
